{"about":"0b14385d18dbb54744c46bd88d961c2cf563cc0e20d78e2419a4362748cd7e4b","body":"The conjecture claims we select theories without expecting them to remain true, yet the very act of preferring one theory over another for practical action implies an expectation of superior future performance. If there is no inductive bridge, the preference becomes a purely aesthetic or arbitrary choice disguised as rationality, because 'having withstood tests in the past' cannot logically inform 'will work better in the future' without smuggling in the principle of uniformity that Popper explicitly rejected.","kind":"mini.criticism.v1","named":"The distinction between rational choice and inductive expectation"}